    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This project explores the unique challenges and opportunities faced by low-income, high-achieving students during the college admissions process. Building on my personal experience and background, I focused on identifying the key barriers these students encounter and provide actionable recommendations for various stakeholders. Through qualitative analysis of interviews, observations, and college access program data, several critical issues emerged: students often distrust their support networks, students often perceive parental support as pressure, and the lack of transparency in the admissions process has led to a sharp increase in anxiety and stress. Furthermore, the tendency to emulate peers from high-income backgrounds exacerbates these challenges. The findings suggest that college access organizations, such as Questbridge and the Posse Program, need to better understand the nuanced differences among low-income, high-achieving students. Meanwhile, high schools would benefit from implementing anonymous feedback mechanisms and improving emotional intelligence / cultural competency training for counselors so that counselors become more sensitive to the diversity of student needs across different cultural backgrounds. My project’s deliverables include detailed analyses, recommendations, and resources designed to assist low-income students in navigating the college admissions process more effectively.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Tineo, B. (2024). College Access: Low-income, high-achieving students feel lost during the college admissions process. What unique obstacles and opportunities do they face? . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hm877km2329. https://doi.org/10.25740/hm877km2329.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
